{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788209622,"nonce":"WOTFPywU6XobsgHOWvHst2uIc0o=","title":"Colony sampling: the harness records no rates, only events — so every leading indicator is unreachable","message":"Wave 4 — learning substrate. Filed by the orchestrator at Evan's direction,\n2026-08-31. Pairs with 4.1 (96d9315); see SEQUENCING below.\n\nTHE GAP\n\nDESIGN already states the problem and does not solve it: \"alerts fire LATE —\ntattered-apparel alert means the mood penalty already landed\", and the 1.6\nissue records the same thing for food (\"`Alert_LowFood` is a lagging indicator,\n`food_days \u003c 3` is a leading one\").\n\nBut a leading indicator is a RATE, and **nothing in the harness samples\nanything.** The journal is events-only BY DESIGN — discrete things that\nhappened, each stamped with tick and seq. It can say \"LowFood fired\". It cannot\nsay \"food has fallen 1.5/day for six days; we reach zero on day 14.\" Those are\ndifferent facts, and only the second one can be acted on early.\n\nThe same hole shows up in every acceptance run. Session 6 measured wood\n700 -\u003e 1077, designations 25 -\u003e 14, stockpile medicine 0 -\u003e 50, steel 33 -\u003e 33.\nEvery one was read-act-read-compare, assembled by hand and thrown away. 5.1's\nrwtest will do that on every assertion it makes. The harness already wants a\ntime series; it is currently kept in the orchestrator's head.\n\nWHAT EVAN NAMED AS THE INDICATORS THAT MATTER\n\nHis framing, 2026-08-31: the game's alert system is largely good enough, WITH\nTHE EXCEPTION of stocking up on **food, medicine, and especially weapons**,\n\"since raids will knock you out\". Weapons is the sharpest of the three because\nno vanilla alert covers armament at all — a colony can field six colonists and\ntwo guns and the game never says a word.\n\nTHE CANDIDATE SAMPLE ROW — colony-level scalars only\n\n    tick, day, season\n    food_days, food_nutrition\n    medicine\n    weapons, weapons_per_colonist\n    steel, wood, components, silver\n    power_gen, power_draw, power_stored\n    colonists_alive, avg_mood, min_mood, downed, injured\n    hostiles_standing, danger_level\n    outdoor_temp, weather\n    alerts_active\n\nPer-pawn detail stays a live drill-down. You do not archive what you can fetch.\n\nVolume, so nobody argues about it from a feeling: one sample per game hour over\na ten-day run is 240 rows; twenty runs is ~4,800 rows and ~106,000 values.\n\nTHE DECISION THAT IS ACTUALLY IRREVERSIBLE — AND IT IS NOT THE STORAGE ENGINE\n\nEvan pushed back on the database idea as slop and was right to. Within one run,\n240 NDJSON lines answer \"is food falling\" with a one-liner; SQLite buys nothing.\nSQL only earns its place at the CROSS-RUN tier — \"what was food_days three days\nbefore each starvation death, and how did the runs that survived differ\" is a\nfilter plus a join to the outcome plus a window offset: one query in SQL, a\nbespoke script every time in NDJSON, which in practice means the question stops\nbeing asked. That tier does not exist until 4.3 has produced multiple runs, and\nNDJSON imports into SQLite any time.\n\n**So do not build a database. Do fix the schema now.** If run 3 records\n`food_days` and run 9 records `food_nutrition` instead, those runs cannot be\ncompared, and the adapting-harness loop dies of schema drift rather than of\nstorage choice. You can pick an engine whenever; you cannot retrofit a\nconsistent column set onto twenty runs that each recorded something different.\n\nSEQUENCING — this should land BEFORE OR WITH 4.1, and here is why\n\n4.1 is \"Playbook: lessons, morning checklist, post-mortems\". Its post-mortem\nhalf is this issue's first consumer. A post-mortem with only the journal can say\n\"we died to a raid\". With samples it can say \"we died to a raid at 14 food-days\nand 0.3 weapons per colonist\". That difference is the whole adapting-harness\nloop: a lesson recording only the CAUSE produces oscillation — died to raids -\u003e\nover-arm -\u003e starve -\u003e over-farm -\u003e die to raids — while a lesson recording the\nTRADEOFF AND ITS COST gives the next run something to interpolate between.\nConvergence in three games instead of forever is a damping problem, and damping\nneeds numbers.\n\nThere is also a process reason. Session 6 recorded that 2.1 (the digest) was\nspecced and BUILT before 4.1, so the code decided what mattered before Evan did.\nThis issue asks the same question a second time — \"what is worth remembering\" is\n\"what is worth looking at\", one tick later. Designing the schema and the morning\nchecklist apart would repeat that mistake exactly.\n\nOPEN QUESTIONS — a research agent is on these; its findings amend this issue\n\n1. **Does RimWorld already record time series?** The History tab draws wealth and\n   population graphs, so `History` / `HistoryAutoRecorder` / `HistoryAutoRecorderDef`\n   record something at some interval. If the game already keeps a value we want,\n   we serialize its recorder rather than building a parallel one — the standing\n   \"serialize, don't reinvent\" rule. This could shrink the whole spec.\n2. **How much of the row is already the digest?** If a sample is mostly \"persist\n   what `DigestVerb` already built\", that is the cheapest possible design.\n3. **Does the game compute food-days**, or is ours a re-derivation?\n4. **Does anything count weapons / weapons-per-colonist**, and is it true that no\n   vanilla alert covers armament?\n5. **Cadence and cost.** `Journal.cs` holds the benchmark to match — 0.0039 ms\n   average per frame, achieved with buffering and an off-thread flush.\n\nHAZARD NOTE, because this one runs 240 times a run\n\nEvery accessor a sampler touches gets read on a schedule, so a write-on-read bug\nhere is multiplied rather than incidental. Five are already catalogued\n(`ResearchManager.GetProgress`, `Zone.Cells`, `Zone_Growing.PlantDefToGrow`,\n`Bill_Production.ShouldDoNow`, and `Bill.ExposeData`'s write-on-SAVE). Every\naccessor this spec adopts is checked against `WorldSafe`/`PawnSafe` discipline\nbefore it is sampled — including the History recorders themselves.\n\nACCEPTANCE\n\n- A sample row is written on a defined cadence during `advance`, with the field\n  set fixed and documented, and the file readable mid-run.\n- Overhead measured against `Journal.cs`'s 0.0039 ms/frame benchmark and\n  reported. If sampling costs materially more, the cadence is justified with the\n  measurement rather than chosen by feel.\n- Every field is marked in the docs as game-recorded, digest-already, or a new\n  derivation, so a reader can see what is re-implemented and what is serialized.\n- A ten-day run produces a series from which `food_days`' SLOPE is computable,\n  demonstrated by reading it back and showing the trend BEFORE the corresponding\n  vanilla alert fires. That is the whole point of the spec and it is the one\n  bullet that must not be met in a weaker form.\n- Zero red errors, and no accessor adopted here mutates on read — demonstrated\n  the way 2.4 did it, with a save-diff around a full sampling window.","files":null}]}